<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karorsinghia Misl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Karorsinghia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karorsinghia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/misl">Misl</a> was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy">Sikh confederacies</a>, or misls, of 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, named after its leader Karora Singh. Its chiefs seized lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> and in the region of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. Its most famous leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Baghel</a> Singh, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1783, and the princely state of Kalsia grew from its holdings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karorsinghia-misl.jpg" alt="Karorsinghia Misl" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Navtej Heer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NORTHERN_INDIA_1780_by_hellbat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
